# Canonical line endings — the LF byte is part of the artifact, not a preference.
#
# Both bundle halves are generated: sources are concatenated and the vendored
# libraries are embedded verbatim as JSON string literals. scripts/build.js now
# normalises every input to LF before assembling, so the artifact is a function of
# the sources and not of the machine that built it. Without `eol=lf` the checkout
# could still disagree with it: `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows (the machine this
# is developed on) turned a fresh clone into a CRLF working tree, `npm run check`
# reported `bundle freshness: src/host.js — stale`, and the Linux runner in CI
# assembled something that could never equal the committed bundle — which is also
# the platform the publish workflow's own gate runs on.
#
# `text=auto` keeps git detecting binary files by content, so the vendored
# raster assets and the logo PNGs are never rewritten.
* text=auto eol=lf
